<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Privacy',
    description: 'LaVioLab Atlas, the plugin for Claude Code and ChatGPT, collects no personal data. What it runs and where it connects.',
  }}
>
  <p>
    <strong>LaVioLab Atlas collects no personal data.</strong> It has no telemetry, no analytics and no
    accounts. The one thing that reaches the author's server is a call to its read-only search tools,
    described below, and nothing from it is stored.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-the-plugin-is">What the plugin is</h2>
  <p>
    LaVioLab Atlas (<code>laviolab-atlas</code>) is a set of instructions for Claude Code: three skills,
    <code>start</code>, <code>content-plan</code> and <code>report</code>, plus one remote MCP server. It
    ships no scripts, binaries or hooks. The skills run on your machine, inside your own Claude Code
    session, and only when you call one of their commands.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp-server">The MCP server</h2>
  <p>
    The server at <code>https://atlas.smolevich.com/mcp</code> has four read-only tools over the published
    pages of this site: <code>search_playbook</code>, <code>get_page</code>, <code>list_sections</code> and
    <code>checklist</code>. It needs no login and has no data about you.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What it receives:</strong> the arguments of a tool call: the words of a search, a page
      slug, a topic, a language. The assistant decides what goes into them; the server never asks for
      your files, your chat or your site address.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What it keeps:</strong> nothing. The server answers from a fixed copy of the atlas and does
      not write the arguments anywhere. The web server in front of it keeps a standard access log for 14
      days: IP address, time, path and user agent, never the request body.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Who it shares with:</strong> nobody. There are no third-party services behind it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="where-it-connects">Where it connects</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The sites you ask it to check.</strong> <code>start</code> and <code>content-plan</code>
      fetch the public pages you name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The atlas MCP server</strong>, when the assistant calls one of its tools, as described above.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nowhere else.</strong> <code>report</code> connects to nothing: it reads the Search Console
      and product exports you download and hand it, and never asks for a password, an API key or an
      access token.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>start</code> never installs anything. When the third-party audits it relies on are missing, it
    prints the install commands and leaves the decision to you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-anthropic-sees">What Claude and Anthropic see</h2>
  <p>
    The plugin runs inside Claude Code, so what you type and what the commands read passes through your
    Claude session under Anthropic's own terms and privacy policy. Besides the MCP server above, the plugin adds no
    destination of its own on top of that.
  </p>

  <h2 id="in-chatgpt">In ChatGPT</h2>
  <p>
    The ChatGPT version has the same three skills and the same MCP server, which is the only place it
    connects to. <code>start</code> reads the
    audit reports you paste or upload, <code>content-plan</code> reads the export you upload, and
    <code>report</code> reads the Search Console and product exports you upload. It never asks for a
    password, an API key or an access token. What you type and upload passes through your ChatGPT
    session under OpenAI's own terms and privacy policy. Besides the MCP server, the plugin adds no
    destination of its own.
  </p>
  <h2 id="this-site">This site</h2>
  <p>
    atlas.smolevich.com is a static site. It sets no cookies of its own and has no accounts or forms.
  </p>

